{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-400",
              "name": "Broken DATABASE_URL deployment locks every authenticated user out of all data APIs (production-wide availability failure)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Broken DATABASE_URL deployment locks every authenticated user out of all data APIs (production-wide availability failure)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The production deployment at https://ecoauditor.io runs with a misconfigured DATABASE_URL: the server's direct Postgres pool cannot reach the database. As a result, ensureCompanyForUser() fails on every authenticated request, no company is ever provisioned, and every authenticated data API returns 403 Forbidden for every user — including paying customers. Dynamic testing against production with two freshly verified accounts confirmed: /api/emissions/summary, /api/emissions/trend, /api/companies/:id/facilities, /api/ingest/csv, /api/calculate, /api/compliance, and /api/reports/* all return {\"success\":false,\"error\":\"Forbidden\"}; /api/trial-status returns {\"source\":\"error-fallback\"}; /api/billing returns 500 \"Failed to load billing state\". The same codebase run locally with a correct DATABASE_URL works end-to-end (company auto-provisioning, CSV ingest, plan gates), proving the root cause is the production environment configuration, not the application logic. Additionally, because subscription state cannot be persisted or read, plan/quota enforcement (requirePlan) silently degrades: requirePlan calls next() whenever loadBillingState returns null, so in this state entitlement checks are non-functional wherever requests do get through."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The production deployment at https://ecoauditor.io runs with a misconfigured DATABASE_URL: the server's direct Postgres pool cannot reach the database. As a result, ensureCompanyForUser() fails on every authenticated request, no company is ever provisioned, and every authenticated data API returns 403 Forbidden for every user — including paying customers. Dynamic testing against production with two freshly verified accounts confirmed: /api/emissions/summary, /api/emissions/trend, /api/companies/:id/facilities, /api/ingest/csv, /api/calculate, /api/compliance, and /api/reports/* all return {\"success\":false,\"error\":\"Forbidden\"}; /api/trial-status returns {\"source\":\"error-fallback\"}; /api/billing returns 500 \"Failed to load billing state\". The same codebase run locally with a correct DATABASE_URL works end-to-end (company auto-provisioning, CSV ingest, plan gates), proving the root cause is the production environment configuration, not the application logic. Additionally, because subscription state cannot be persisted or read, plan/quota enforcement (requirePlan) silently degrades: requirePlan calls next() whenever loadBillingState returns null, so in this state entitlement checks are non-functional wherever requests do get through.\n\nComplete denial of service for the paid product: no authenticated user can view dashboards, import emissions data, calculate emissions, create facilities, or generate/download reports on the production site. Billing state is unreadable (trial/subscription status unknown to users and unenforceable), so revenue-critical entitlement enforcement is degraded and customers who pay via Stripe checkout would not have their subscription synced to any usable application state. GDPR consent-record persistence also fails (500s) instead of recording consent, creating a compliance record-keeping gap.\n\n1. Fix the production environment: set DATABASE_URL to a reachable Postgres connection string (the InsForge project's direct Postgres endpoint) in the Railway service variables and redeploy; verify a single authenticated request returns 200/402 rather than 403. 2. Add startup validation: when INSFORGE_BASE_URL or DATABASE_URL is configured, perform a one-time SELECT 1 at boot and log FATAL / exit non-zero if the DB is unreachable so the deploy fails loudly instead of serving 403s to all users. 3. Change /api/health and /health to probe the database (SELECT 1 with a short timeout) and report db:\"ok\"/\"unreachable\" instead of echoing env-var presence. 4. Make requirePlan fail closed for plan-gated routes: when billing state cannot be loaded due to an error (as opposed to company-not-yet-provisioned), return 503 instead of next(). 5. Add an uptime/synthetic check that exercises one authenticated data endpoint and alerts on 403 spikes.",
                "markdown": "The production deployment at https://ecoauditor.io runs with a misconfigured DATABASE_URL: the server's direct Postgres pool cannot reach the database. As a result, ensureCompanyForUser() fails on every authenticated request, no company is ever provisioned, and every authenticated data API returns 403 Forbidden for every user — including paying customers. Dynamic testing against production with two freshly verified accounts confirmed: /api/emissions/summary, /api/emissions/trend, /api/companies/:id/facilities, /api/ingest/csv, /api/calculate, /api/compliance, and /api/reports/* all return {\"success\":false,\"error\":\"Forbidden\"}; /api/trial-status returns {\"source\":\"error-fallback\"}; /api/billing returns 500 \"Failed to load billing state\". The same codebase run locally with a correct DATABASE_URL works end-to-end (company auto-provisioning, CSV ingest, plan gates), proving the root cause is the production environment configuration, not the application logic. Additionally, because subscription state cannot be persisted or read, plan/quota enforcement (requirePlan) silently degrades: requirePlan calls next() whenever loadBillingState returns null, so in this state entitlement checks are non-functional wherever requests do get through.\n\nComplete denial of service for the paid product: no authenticated user can view dashboards, import emissions data, calculate emissions, create facilities, or generate/download reports on the production site. Billing state is unreadable (trial/subscription status unknown to users and unenforceable), so revenue-critical entitlement enforcement is degraded and customers who pay via Stripe checkout would not have their subscription synced to any usable application state. GDPR consent-record persistence also fails (500s) instead of recording consent, creating a compliance record-keeping gap.\n\n1. Fix the production environment: set DATABASE_URL to a reachable Postgres connection string (the InsForge project's direct Postgres endpoint) in the Railway service variables and redeploy; verify a single authenticated request returns 200/402 rather than 403. 2. Add startup validation: when INSFORGE_BASE_URL or DATABASE_URL is configured, perform a one-time SELECT 1 at boot and log FATAL / exit non-zero if the DB is unreachable so the deploy fails loudly instead of serving 403s to all users. 3. Change /api/health and /health to probe the database (SELECT 1 with a short timeout) and report db:\"ok\"/\"unreachable\" instead of echoing env-var presence. 4. Make requirePlan fail closed for plan-gated routes: when billing state cannot be loaded due to an error (as opposed to company-not-yet-provisioned), return 503 instead of next(). 5. Add an uptime/synthetic check that exercises one authenticated data endpoint and alerts on 403 spikes."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-400",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-863",
              "name": "requirePlan middleware fails open on billing-check errors, granting plan-gated features to expired/canceled accounts",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "requirePlan middleware fails open on billing-check errors, granting plan-gated features to expired/canceled accounts"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The requirePlan() middleware in server.cjs wraps the billing lookup in a try/catch and calls next() on any error (\"fail-open: a billing check outage must not take down core APIs\"). It also calls next() when loadBillingState returns null. Dynamically confirmed against a local instance with a real auth stub: a user whose trial had expired (correctly receiving 402 upgrade_required) received HTTP 200 with full data from GET /api/emissions/summary — the moment the billing query raised an error (simulated schema drift: a dropped billing column, exactly the class of outage the comment anticipates). The server log shows \"requirePlan check failed\" followed by the request being served. In the currently-misconfigured production state (broken DATABASE_URL), this fail-open compounds the outage: anywhere a request reaches a plan-gated route with a null billing state, entitlement enforcement is silently skipped."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The requirePlan() middleware in server.cjs wraps the billing lookup in a try/catch and calls next() on any error (\"fail-open: a billing check outage must not take down core APIs\"). It also calls next() when loadBillingState returns null. Dynamically confirmed against a local instance with a real auth stub: a user whose trial had expired (correctly receiving 402 upgrade_required) received HTTP 200 with full data from GET /api/emissions/summary — the moment the billing query raised an error (simulated schema drift: a dropped billing column, exactly the class of outage the comment anticipates). The server log shows \"requirePlan check failed\" followed by the request being served. In the currently-misconfigured production state (broken DATABASE_URL), this fail-open compounds the outage: anywhere a request reaches a plan-gated route with a null billing state, entitlement enforcement is silently skipped.\n\nDuring any database/billing-check outage (connection failure, schema drift, permission error), all plan enforcement vanishes: expired-trial and canceled users get full access to emissions dashboards, CSV imports, report generation, Scope 3 calculations and report downloads that should be paywalled. Entitlement integrity is lost precisely when the system is degraded, and quota limits (facility caps, monthly CSV import counts, Scope 3 gating) become unenforceable.\n\n1. In requirePlan, distinguish \"billing lookup error\" from \"no billing state\": on a thrown error, respond 503 {\"error\":\"Billing status unavailable, please retry\"} instead of next(). 2. When loadBillingState returns null but a company row exists, treat as inactive (402), not entitled; only the not-yet-provisioned first-access case should pass through (and that case should be narrowed to company-provisioning routes). 3. Cache the last known-good billing state briefly (e.g. 60s per user) so transient DB blips do not flip enforcement behavior. 4. Add a regression test: induce a loadBillingState error and assert plan-gated routes return 503, plus a test that expired-trial users are denied during partial DB outages.",
                "markdown": "The requirePlan() middleware in server.cjs wraps the billing lookup in a try/catch and calls next() on any error (\"fail-open: a billing check outage must not take down core APIs\"). It also calls next() when loadBillingState returns null. Dynamically confirmed against a local instance with a real auth stub: a user whose trial had expired (correctly receiving 402 upgrade_required) received HTTP 200 with full data from GET /api/emissions/summary — the moment the billing query raised an error (simulated schema drift: a dropped billing column, exactly the class of outage the comment anticipates). The server log shows \"requirePlan check failed\" followed by the request being served. In the currently-misconfigured production state (broken DATABASE_URL), this fail-open compounds the outage: anywhere a request reaches a plan-gated route with a null billing state, entitlement enforcement is silently skipped.\n\nDuring any database/billing-check outage (connection failure, schema drift, permission error), all plan enforcement vanishes: expired-trial and canceled users get full access to emissions dashboards, CSV imports, report generation, Scope 3 calculations and report downloads that should be paywalled. Entitlement integrity is lost precisely when the system is degraded, and quota limits (facility caps, monthly CSV import counts, Scope 3 gating) become unenforceable.\n\n1. In requirePlan, distinguish \"billing lookup error\" from \"no billing state\": on a thrown error, respond 503 {\"error\":\"Billing status unavailable, please retry\"} instead of next(). 2. When loadBillingState returns null but a company row exists, treat as inactive (402), not entitled; only the not-yet-provisioned first-access case should pass through (and that case should be narrowed to company-provisioning routes). 3. Cache the last known-good billing state briefly (e.g. 60s per user) so transient DB blips do not flip enforcement behavior. 4. Add a regression test: induce a loadBillingState error and assert plan-gated routes return 503, plus a test that expired-trial users are denied during partial DB outages."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-863",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/863.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Hard-coded InsForge API key and Ollama API key committed to the repository",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hard-coded InsForge API key and Ollama API key committed to the repository"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two live-format secrets are committed to the git repository. (1) opencode.json contains InsForge API key \"ik_[REDACTED-ROTATED-2026-09]\" for backend https://insforge-production-0d5a.up.railway.app, committed in the tracked file opencode.json (present in the current HEAD). (2) Git history commit edfa108 (\"config: add ollama api key for openensemble integration\") added .env.ollama containing OLLAMA_API_KEY=[REDACTED-REVOKED-2026-09] Both were found by automated secret scanning (gitleaks) and verified by reading the files/commits directly. The InsForge key was tested against its configured backend (returns 404 application-not-found — backend appears decommissioned) and against the current production InsForge project (rejected as invalid), so present-day exploitability is limited; however committed keys remain valid attack material if either backend is restored or the keys were reused, and their presence in history violates the project's own \"never hardcode or commit keys\" policy (AGENTS.md)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Two live-format secrets are committed to the git repository. (1) opencode.json contains InsForge API key \"ik_[REDACTED-ROTATED-2026-09]\" for backend https://insforge-production-0d5a.up.railway.app, committed in the tracked file opencode.json (present in the current HEAD). (2) Git history commit edfa108 (\"config: add ollama api key for openensemble integration\") added .env.ollama containing OLLAMA_API_KEY=[REDACTED-REVOKED-2026-09] Both were found by automated secret scanning (gitleaks) and verified by reading the files/commits directly. The InsForge key was tested against its configured backend (returns 404 application-not-found — backend appears decommissioned) and against the current production InsForge project (rejected as invalid), so present-day exploitability is limited; however committed keys remain valid attack material if either backend is restored or the keys were reused, and their presence in history violates the project's own \"never hardcode or commit keys\" policy (AGENTS.md).\n\nAnyone with read access to the repository (including any future public exposure or clone) obtains long-lived API credentials. If the associated backends are reachable with these keys, an attacker could read/modify backend data or consume paid LLM quota at the owner's expense. Even while the backends appear decommissioned, keys in git history are permanently exposed and must be rotated; the pattern also normalizes committing secrets, which previously leaked provider credentials (insforge.toml history).\n\n1. Rotate both credentials immediately (revoke the InsForge API key in the InsForge dashboard; revoke/regenerate the Ollama API key) even though the backends appear decommissioned. 2. Remove the secrets from the repository: replace the API_KEY value in opencode.json with an environment-variable reference (e.g. \"${INSFORGE_API_KEY}\") and purge .env.ollama from history (git filter-repo or BFG), then force-push and ask all clones to re-clone. 3. Add opencode.json's env section and *.env.ollama to .gitignore or move MCP credentials to a local-only config. 4. Add a pre-commit/CI secret-scanning gate (gitleaks action) so future keys are blocked at commit time. 5. Audit the InsForge dashboard access logs for any use of the exposed key.",
                "markdown": "Two live-format secrets are committed to the git repository. (1) opencode.json contains InsForge API key \"ik_[REDACTED-ROTATED-2026-09]\" for backend https://insforge-production-0d5a.up.railway.app, committed in the tracked file opencode.json (present in the current HEAD). (2) Git history commit edfa108 (\"config: add ollama api key for openensemble integration\") added .env.ollama containing OLLAMA_API_KEY=[REDACTED-REVOKED-2026-09] Both were found by automated secret scanning (gitleaks) and verified by reading the files/commits directly. The InsForge key was tested against its configured backend (returns 404 application-not-found — backend appears decommissioned) and against the current production InsForge project (rejected as invalid), so present-day exploitability is limited; however committed keys remain valid attack material if either backend is restored or the keys were reused, and their presence in history violates the project's own \"never hardcode or commit keys\" policy (AGENTS.md).\n\nAnyone with read access to the repository (including any future public exposure or clone) obtains long-lived API credentials. If the associated backends are reachable with these keys, an attacker could read/modify backend data or consume paid LLM quota at the owner's expense. Even while the backends appear decommissioned, keys in git history are permanently exposed and must be rotated; the pattern also normalizes committing secrets, which previously leaked provider credentials (insforge.toml history).\n\n1. Rotate both credentials immediately (revoke the InsForge API key in the InsForge dashboard; revoke/regenerate the Ollama API key) even though the backends appear decommissioned. 2. Remove the secrets from the repository: replace the API_KEY value in opencode.json with an environment-variable reference (e.g. \"${INSFORGE_API_KEY}\") and purge .env.ollama from history (git filter-repo or BFG), then force-push and ask all clones to re-clone. 3. Add opencode.json's env section and *.env.ollama to .gitignore or move MCP credentials to a local-only config. 4. Add a pre-commit/CI secret-scanning gate (gitleaks action) so future keys are blocked at commit time. 5. Audit the InsForge dashboard access logs for any use of the exposed key."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-306",
              "name": "Public LLM fallback in /api/chat enables unauthenticated AI quota consumption and prompt injection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Public LLM fallback in /api/chat enables unauthenticated AI quota consumption and prompt injection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "POST /api/chat is a public, unauthenticated endpoint. When the sales-bot regex engine does not match a message, the server forwards the full user message to an upstream LLM (OpenAI gpt-4o-mini or Anthropic claude-sonnet-4, whichever API key is configured) and returns the generated text. There is no authentication, no per-session budget, and the only throttle is the global 120 req/min per-IP limit. The system prompt is generic and there is no output filtering, so the endpoint is a free, anonymous LLM proxy: arbitrary prompts are answered (verified the code path and response plumbing; the regex path is trivially bypassed by sending any off-topic message). An attacker can consume the owner's paid LLM quota at scale by rotating source IPs, and can use prompt injection to make the assistant produce off-brand or harmful content that is then attributed to the product's chat widget. Additionally, the client-controlled `state` object is trusted for the demo-booking flow steps (though lead fields are sanitized before persistence, mitigating stored-data injection)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "POST /api/chat is a public, unauthenticated endpoint. When the sales-bot regex engine does not match a message, the server forwards the full user message to an upstream LLM (OpenAI gpt-4o-mini or Anthropic claude-sonnet-4, whichever API key is configured) and returns the generated text. There is no authentication, no per-session budget, and the only throttle is the global 120 req/min per-IP limit. The system prompt is generic and there is no output filtering, so the endpoint is a free, anonymous LLM proxy: arbitrary prompts are answered (verified the code path and response plumbing; the regex path is trivially bypassed by sending any off-topic message). An attacker can consume the owner's paid LLM quota at scale by rotating source IPs, and can use prompt injection to make the assistant produce off-brand or harmful content that is then attributed to the product's chat widget. Additionally, the client-controlled `state` object is trusted for the demo-booking flow steps (though lead fields are sanitized before persistence, mitigating stored-data injection).\n\nDirect financial loss through unauthorized consumption of OpenAI/Anthropic API quota (any anonymous visitor can generate LLM completions billed to the site owner). Prompt-injection abuse can make the official chat widget produce misleading, off-policy, or defamatory answers under the product's brand, and the endpoint can be used as a laundering proxy for LLM access. During high-volume abuse, legitimate chat users experience degraded service.\n\n1. Gate the LLM fallback behind authentication (authGuard) or behind a verified session token issued to the widget after a proof-of-work/captcha challenge. 2. Apply the existing perRouteRateLimit pattern to /api/chat with a small budget (e.g. 10-20 req/min/IP) plus a daily per-IP and global circuit-breaker cap on LLM calls; stop calling the LLM when the cap trips. 3. Harden the system prompt against instruction override and filter/validate LLM output before returning it (block responses that ignore the sales/support domain). 4. Consider removing the free-form LLM fallback entirely and answering with the static KB + contact form, which already covers the widget's sales purpose. 5. Add spend alerting on the OpenAI/Anthropic accounts to detect abuse quickly.",
                "markdown": "POST /api/chat is a public, unauthenticated endpoint. When the sales-bot regex engine does not match a message, the server forwards the full user message to an upstream LLM (OpenAI gpt-4o-mini or Anthropic claude-sonnet-4, whichever API key is configured) and returns the generated text. There is no authentication, no per-session budget, and the only throttle is the global 120 req/min per-IP limit. The system prompt is generic and there is no output filtering, so the endpoint is a free, anonymous LLM proxy: arbitrary prompts are answered (verified the code path and response plumbing; the regex path is trivially bypassed by sending any off-topic message). An attacker can consume the owner's paid LLM quota at scale by rotating source IPs, and can use prompt injection to make the assistant produce off-brand or harmful content that is then attributed to the product's chat widget. Additionally, the client-controlled `state` object is trusted for the demo-booking flow steps (though lead fields are sanitized before persistence, mitigating stored-data injection).\n\nDirect financial loss through unauthorized consumption of OpenAI/Anthropic API quota (any anonymous visitor can generate LLM completions billed to the site owner). Prompt-injection abuse can make the official chat widget produce misleading, off-policy, or defamatory answers under the product's brand, and the endpoint can be used as a laundering proxy for LLM access. During high-volume abuse, legitimate chat users experience degraded service.\n\n1. Gate the LLM fallback behind authentication (authGuard) or behind a verified session token issued to the widget after a proof-of-work/captcha challenge. 2. Apply the existing perRouteRateLimit pattern to /api/chat with a small budget (e.g. 10-20 req/min/IP) plus a daily per-IP and global circuit-breaker cap on LLM calls; stop calling the LLM when the cap trips. 3. Harden the system prompt against instruction override and filter/validate LLM output before returning it (block responses that ignore the sales/support domain). 4. Consider removing the free-form LLM fallback entirely and answering with the static KB + contact form, which already covers the widget's sales purpose. 5. Add spend alerting on the OpenAI/Anthropic accounts to detect abuse quickly."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0005",
              "name": "Vulnerable dependencies: react-router 7.17.0 (DoS, open redirect, CSRF bypass), postcss 8.5.14 path traversal, ws 8.20.1 DoS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Vulnerable dependencies: react-router 7.17.0 (DoS, open redirect, CSRF bypass), postcss 8.5.14 path traversal, ws 8.20.1 DoS"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Dependency scanning (trivy fs) of package-lock.json identified 9 known vulnerabilities in shipped dependencies. High severity: (1) GHSA-r28c-9q8g-f849 — postcss 8.5.14 path traversal in previous source map auto-loading (fixed 8.5.18); (2) CVE-2026-55685 — react-router 7.17.0 unauthenticated denial of service via inefficient route matching (fixed 7.18.0); (3) GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 — react-router RSC-mode CSRF bypass allowing action execution before the 400 response (fixed 8.3.0); (4) CVE-2026-48779 — ws 8.20.1 denial of service via memory exhaustion from small WebSocket fragments (fixed 8.21.0). Medium: react-router information disclosure via client-side constructor (CVE-2026-53666) and untrusted redirects / open redirect via missing protocol validation and backslash navigation (CVE-2026-53667, CVE-2026-53669 — fixed 7.18.0). Low: body-parser invalid-limit DoS (CVE-2026-12590), esbuild dev-server file read on Windows (GHSA-g7r4-m6w7-qqqr). react-router is a direct production dependency used for all client-side routing, so its routing/redirect flaws are directly exposed in the SPA."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Dependency scanning (trivy fs) of package-lock.json identified 9 known vulnerabilities in shipped dependencies. High severity: (1) GHSA-r28c-9q8g-f849 — postcss 8.5.14 path traversal in previous source map auto-loading (fixed 8.5.18); (2) CVE-2026-55685 — react-router 7.17.0 unauthenticated denial of service via inefficient route matching (fixed 7.18.0); (3) GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 — react-router RSC-mode CSRF bypass allowing action execution before the 400 response (fixed 8.3.0); (4) CVE-2026-48779 — ws 8.20.1 denial of service via memory exhaustion from small WebSocket fragments (fixed 8.21.0). Medium: react-router information disclosure via client-side constructor (CVE-2026-53666) and untrusted redirects / open redirect via missing protocol validation and backslash navigation (CVE-2026-53667, CVE-2026-53669 — fixed 7.18.0). Low: body-parser invalid-limit DoS (CVE-2026-12590), esbuild dev-server file read on Windows (GHSA-g7r4-m6w7-qqqr). react-router is a direct production dependency used for all client-side routing, so its routing/redirect flaws are directly exposed in the SPA.\n\nDepending on the CVE: unauthenticated denial of service against the application's routing layer, open redirects that enable phishing from the trusted ecoauditor.io domain, information disclosure of route constructor details, path traversal during the build (postcss source maps), and WebSocket memory-exhaustion DoS where ws is used. Open-redirect and DoS issues in react-router are directly reachable in the deployed single-page application.\n\n1. Upgrade react-router to >=7.18.0 (or 8.3.0 if adopting v8) and regenerate package-lock.json. 2. Bump the pinned postcss from 8.5.14 to >=8.5.18 in package.json. 3. Upgrade ws to >=8.21.0 and body-parser to >=1.20.6 (express ^4.22.1 chain) via lockfile refresh. 4. Pin esbuild to the fixed 0.28.1+ (existing override only needs a patch bump). 5. Add npm audit / trivy fs to CI with a severity gate (fail on HIGH) and enable Dependabot/Renovate for routine updates. 6. Re-run trivy fs after upgrades and confirm zero HIGH findings.",
                "markdown": "Dependency scanning (trivy fs) of package-lock.json identified 9 known vulnerabilities in shipped dependencies. High severity: (1) GHSA-r28c-9q8g-f849 — postcss 8.5.14 path traversal in previous source map auto-loading (fixed 8.5.18); (2) CVE-2026-55685 — react-router 7.17.0 unauthenticated denial of service via inefficient route matching (fixed 7.18.0); (3) GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 — react-router RSC-mode CSRF bypass allowing action execution before the 400 response (fixed 8.3.0); (4) CVE-2026-48779 — ws 8.20.1 denial of service via memory exhaustion from small WebSocket fragments (fixed 8.21.0). Medium: react-router information disclosure via client-side constructor (CVE-2026-53666) and untrusted redirects / open redirect via missing protocol validation and backslash navigation (CVE-2026-53667, CVE-2026-53669 — fixed 7.18.0). Low: body-parser invalid-limit DoS (CVE-2026-12590), esbuild dev-server file read on Windows (GHSA-g7r4-m6w7-qqqr). react-router is a direct production dependency used for all client-side routing, so its routing/redirect flaws are directly exposed in the SPA.\n\nDepending on the CVE: unauthenticated denial of service against the application's routing layer, open redirects that enable phishing from the trusted ecoauditor.io domain, information disclosure of route constructor details, path traversal during the build (postcss source maps), and WebSocket memory-exhaustion DoS where ws is used. Open-redirect and DoS issues in react-router are directly reachable in the deployed single-page application.\n\n1. Upgrade react-router to >=7.18.0 (or 8.3.0 if adopting v8) and regenerate package-lock.json. 2. Bump the pinned postcss from 8.5.14 to >=8.5.18 in package.json. 3. Upgrade ws to >=8.21.0 and body-parser to >=1.20.6 (express ^4.22.1 chain) via lockfile refresh. 4. Pin esbuild to the fixed 0.28.1+ (existing override only needs a patch bump). 5. Add npm audit / trivy fs to CI with a severity gate (fail on HIGH) and enable Dependabot/Renovate for routine updates. 6. Re-run trivy fs after upgrades and confirm zero HIGH findings."
              },
              "properties": {
