        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-400",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Broken DATABASE_URL deployment locks every authenticated user out of all data APIs (production-wide availability failure)\n\nThe production deployment at https://ecoauditor.io runs with a misconfigured DATABASE_URL: the server's direct Postgres pool cannot reach the database. As a result, ensureCompanyForUser() fails on every authenticated request, no company is ever provisioned, and every authenticated data API returns 403 Forbidden for every user — including paying customers. Dynamic testing against production with two freshly verified accounts confirmed: /api/emissions/summary, /api/emissions/trend, /api/companies/:id/facilities, /api/ingest/csv, /api/calculate, /api/compliance, and /api/reports/* all return {\"success\":false,\"error\":\"Forbidden\"}; /api/trial-status returns {\"source\":\"error-fallback\"}; /api/billing returns 500 \"Failed to load billing state\". The same codebase run locally with a correct DATABASE_URL works end-to-end (company auto-provisioning, CSV ingest, plan gates), proving the root cause is the production environment configuration, not the application logic. Additionally, because subscription state cannot be persisted or read, plan/quota enforcement (requirePlan) silently degrades: requirePlan calls next() whenever loadBillingState returns null, so in this state entitlement checks are non-functional wherever requests do get through."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/emissions/summary (and all authenticated /api/* data routes)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "ed846c4380684077514b844aa74028662baded9cda9a7c2d5da97586c3a64a57"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "1c4bec641ead1eae2fffbab1db0c84ec8899f369c0be79b216d9e6a9bcff6d1e",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-07-29 04:10:12 UTC",
              "target": "https://ecoauditor.io (deployment of /workspace/eco-auditor, server.cjs)",
              "endpoint": "/api/emissions/summary (and all authenticated /api/* data routes)",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-400",
              "impact": "Complete denial of service for the paid product: no authenticated user can view dashboards, import emissions data, calculate emissions, create facilities, or generate/download reports on the production site. Billing state is unreadable (trial/subscription status unknown to users and unenforceable), so revenue-critical entitlement enforcement is degraded and customers who pay via Stripe checkout would not have their subscription synced to any usable application state. GDPR consent-record persistence also fails (500s) instead of recording consent, creating a compliance record-keeping gap.",
              "technical_analysis": "server.cjs builds pgPool only when DATABASE_URL is set (server.cjs ~line 55), and every tenant-scoped query depends on it. requireCompanyAccess -> ensureCompanyForUser issues BEGIN / SET LOCAL row_security = off / SELECT ... FROM public.companies; in production this connection fails (DNS/target unreachable or wrong URL), the error is swallowed (returns null), and resolveAuthorizedCompanyId then finds no company claims on the user, yielding 403 for 100% of authenticated traffic. Verified by reproducing the exact failure modes locally: with a working DATABASE_URL all flows succeed; with a broken one the identical 403/error-fallback/500 signatures appear. The deployment's own /api/health reports db:\"configured\" (env var present) masking the fact the DB is unreachable — the health check only tests env presence, not connectivity. Root causes: (1) no startup validation that DATABASE_URL is reachable (HUMAN_ACTIONS.md item AUTH-2 already proposed fail-fast but it was not implemented); (2) /api/health db status checks env presence instead of a live probe; (3) requirePlan fail-open when billing state is null.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Fix the production environment: set DATABASE_URL to a reachable Postgres connection string (the InsForge project's direct Postgres endpoint) in the Railway service variables and redeploy; verify a single authenticated request returns 200/402 rather than 403. 2. Add startup validation: when INSFORGE_BASE_URL or DATABASE_URL is configured, perform a one-time SELECT 1 at boot and log FATAL / exit non-zero if the DB is unreachable so the deploy fails loudly instead of serving 403s to all users. 3. Change /api/health and /health to probe the database (SELECT 1 with a short timeout) and report db:\"ok\"/\"unreachable\" instead of echoing env-var presence. 4. Make requirePlan fail closed for plan-gated routes: when billing state cannot be loaded due to an error (as opposed to company-not-yet-provisioned), return 503 instead of next(). 5. Add an uptime/synthetic check that exercises one authenticated data endpoint and alerts on 403 spikes.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Register a fresh account via the InsForge auth backend (POST /api/auth/users with the public anon key from the deployed bundle), retrieve the verification code, and complete email verification (POST /api/auth/email/verify) to obtain an accessToken. 2. Call production endpoints with that token: GET https://ecoauditor.io/api/emissions/summary with Authorization: Bearer <token> returns 403 {\"success\":false,\"error\":\"Forbidden\"}. 3. GET /api/trial-status returns {\"trial\":true,\"trialEndsAt\":null,\"source\":\"error-fallback\"} (server log shows \"Trial status check failed\"). 4. GET /api/billing returns HTTP 500 {\"error\":\"Failed to load billing state\"}. 5. POST /api/ingest/csv, POST /api/calculate, GET /api/companies/1/facilities all return the same 403 Forbidden for both test accounts, proving no user can ever be provisioned. 6. Run the same server.cjs locally with a correct DATABASE_URL — identical requests succeed (200 with data), isolating the defect to production configuration.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "requirePlan middleware fails open on billing-check errors, granting plan-gated features to expired/canceled accounts\n\nThe requirePlan() middleware in server.cjs wraps the billing lookup in a try/catch and calls next() on any error (\"fail-open: a billing check outage must not take down core APIs\"). It also calls next() when loadBillingState returns null. Dynamically confirmed against a local instance with a real auth stub: a user whose trial had expired (correctly receiving 402 upgrade_required) received HTTP 200 with full data from GET /api/emissions/summary — the moment the billing query raised an error (simulated schema drift: a dropped billing column, exactly the class of outage the comment anticipates). The server log shows \"requirePlan check failed\" followed by the request being served. In the currently-misconfigured production state (broken DATABASE_URL), this fail-open compounds the outage: anywhere a request reaches a plan-gated route with a null billing state, entitlement enforcement is silently skipped."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/emissions/summary (all requirePlan-gated routes)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "9accedd21d613f2bafdb1e5128c8b89f6662c1fe87b606b621333d99bf5544ce"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.2",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "5ce6bf2ff242c336e8124bc5fbdbc0ae15ea29e249f1aa2704101a4da7fec8cf",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.2,
              "timestamp": "2026-07-29 04:11:10 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/eco-auditor (server.cjs requirePlan middleware) — affects https://ecoauditor.io deployment",
              "endpoint": "/api/emissions/summary (all requirePlan-gated routes)",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-863",
              "impact": "During any database/billing-check outage (connection failure, schema drift, permission error), all plan enforcement vanishes: expired-trial and canceled users get full access to emissions dashboards, CSV imports, report generation, Scope 3 calculations and report downloads that should be paywalled. Entitlement integrity is lost precisely when the system is degraded, and quota limits (facility caps, monthly CSV import counts, Scope 3 gating) become unenforceable.",
              "technical_analysis": "requirePlan(minPlanId) in server.cjs loads billing state via loadBillingState(req.user.id). Two bypass paths exist: (1) catch (err) -> log + return next() — any thrown error (DB down, missing column, RLS issue) grants access; (2) if (!state) return next() — null state (no company row or DB returning no rows) also grants access. Verified dynamically: baseline expired-trial user -> 402; drop column public.companies.subscription_billing_cycle so loadBillingState throws -> same request returns 200 with emissions data; restore column -> 402 again. Root cause is a deliberate fail-open comment, but it conflates \"billing check outage\" with \"user is entitled\": the safe degraded behavior for a paywall is 503, not silent access.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. In requirePlan, distinguish \"billing lookup error\" from \"no billing state\": on a thrown error, respond 503 {\"error\":\"Billing status unavailable, please retry\"} instead of next(). 2. When loadBillingState returns null but a company row exists, treat as inactive (402), not entitled; only the not-yet-provisioned first-access case should pass through (and that case should be narrowed to company-provisioning routes). 3. Cache the last known-good billing state briefly (e.g. 60s per user) so transient DB blips do not flip enforcement behavior. 4. Add a regression test: induce a loadBillingState error and assert plan-gated routes return 503, plus a test that expired-trial users are denied during partial DB outages.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Run server.cjs with DATABASE_URL set and an InsForge-compatible auth stub. 2. Provision company id=1 for the test user, then set trial_ends_at to the past; confirm GET /api/emissions/summary returns 402 upgrade_required. 3. Induce a billing-check error (e.g. ALTER TABLE public.companies DROP COLUMN subscription_billing_cycle — simulating schema drift). 4. Repeat GET /api/emissions/summary with the same token: server logs \"requirePlan check failed\" and responds HTTP 200 with the full emissions summary. 5. Restore the column: the endpoint returns 402 again, proving access was granted by the fail-open path.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-798",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Hard-coded InsForge API key and Ollama API key committed to the repository\n\nTwo live-format secrets are committed to the git repository. (1) opencode.json contains InsForge API key \"ik_[REDACTED-ROTATED-2026-09]\" for backend https://insforge-production-0d5a.up.railway.app, committed in the tracked file opencode.json (present in the current HEAD). (2) Git history commit edfa108 (\"config: add ollama api key for openensemble integration\") added .env.ollama containing OLLAMA_API_KEY=[REDACTED-REVOKED-2026-09] Both were found by automated secret scanning (gitleaks) and verified by reading the files/commits directly. The InsForge key was tested against its configured backend (returns 404 application-not-found — backend appears decommissioned) and against the current production InsForge project (rejected as invalid), so present-day exploitability is limited; however committed keys remain valid attack material if either backend is restored or the keys were reused, and their presence in history violates the project's own \"never hardcode or commit keys\" policy (AGENTS.md)."
          },
