          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "cf100e221b79e64b3fac1b6dfdeb6e3f8b2fb2be52af8dee108d8cdf06e5ca9c",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-07-29 04:11:59 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/eco-auditor (git repository)",
              "cwe": "CWE-798",
              "impact": "Anyone with read access to the repository (including any future public exposure or clone) obtains long-lived API credentials. If the associated backends are reachable with these keys, an attacker could read/modify backend data or consume paid LLM quota at the owner's expense. Even while the backends appear decommissioned, keys in git history are permanently exposed and must be rotated; the pattern also normalizes committing secrets, which previously leaked provider credentials (insforge.toml history).",
              "technical_analysis": "gitleaks detect flagged 2063 findings; filtering tool-state noise left 3 real items: opencode.json line 11 (InsForge ik_ key, tracked in HEAD), tests/insforge.test.ts (dummy JWT, benign), and git history file .env.ollama (Ollama API key, commit edfa108). The InsForge key grants API access to the MCP-configured backend URL embedded next to it. Verification: POST/GET with the key against insforge-production-0d5a.up.railway.app returns 404 application-not-found (decommissioned); against the current production InsForge project (53s6nmc5.us-east.insforge.app) the key is rejected (401 Invalid API key), confirming it is not the current project's key but remains a real committed credential. Root cause: developer tooling config (opencode.json MCP env, .env.ollama) committed with inline secrets instead of environment indirection.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Rotate both credentials immediately (revoke the InsForge API key in the InsForge dashboard; revoke/regenerate the Ollama API key) even though the backends appear decommissioned. 2. Remove the secrets from the repository: replace the API_KEY value in opencode.json with an environment-variable reference (e.g. \"${INSFORGE_API_KEY}\") and purge .env.ollama from history (git filter-repo or BFG), then force-push and ask all clones to re-clone. 3. Add opencode.json's env section and *.env.ollama to .gitignore or move MCP credentials to a local-only config. 4. Add a pre-commit/CI secret-scanning gate (gitleaks action) so future keys are blocked at commit time. 5. Audit the InsForge dashboard access logs for any use of the exposed key.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Run gitleaks detect --source . against the repository — it reports the ik_ key in opencode.json and the Ollama key in historical .env.ollama. 2. Read opencode.json in the working tree: the API_KEY value is present in cleartext. 3. git show edfa108 displays .env.ollama with OLLAMA_API_KEY in cleartext. 4. Test the InsForge key: curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer ik_sehr...\" https://insforge-production-0d5a.up.railway.app/api/auth/sessions/current returns 404 (app decommissioned), demonstrating the key is a real credential bound to a live URL, only currently inert because the deployment is gone.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
