        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Public LLM fallback in /api/chat enables unauthenticated AI quota consumption and prompt injection\n\nPOST /api/chat is a public, unauthenticated endpoint. When the sales-bot regex engine does not match a message, the server forwards the full user message to an upstream LLM (OpenAI gpt-4o-mini or Anthropic claude-sonnet-4, whichever API key is configured) and returns the generated text. There is no authentication, no per-session budget, and the only throttle is the global 120 req/min per-IP limit. The system prompt is generic and there is no output filtering, so the endpoint is a free, anonymous LLM proxy: arbitrary prompts are answered (verified the code path and response plumbing; the regex path is trivially bypassed by sending any off-topic message). An attacker can consume the owner's paid LLM quota at scale by rotating source IPs, and can use prompt injection to make the assistant produce off-brand or harmful content that is then attributed to the product's chat widget. Additionally, the client-controlled `state` object is trusted for the demo-booking flow steps (though lead fields are sanitized before persistence, mitigating stored-data injection)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/chat",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "4e15e5e8e63ce643744ffd7b4bef14d39251ca1b859c2158b39c51eb4c3e1418"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "28310f196f665eb1fd81f243fec04216ab415e0efb8d7cbf971cf31b5730f518",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-07-29 04:12:56 UTC",
              "target": "https://ecoauditor.io/api/chat (server.cjs /api/chat route)",
              "endpoint": "/api/chat",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-306",
              "impact": "Direct financial loss through unauthorized consumption of OpenAI/Anthropic API quota (any anonymous visitor can generate LLM completions billed to the site owner). Prompt-injection abuse can make the official chat widget produce misleading, off-policy, or defamatory answers under the product's brand, and the endpoint can be used as a laundering proxy for LLM access. During high-volume abuse, legitimate chat users experience degraded service.",
              "technical_analysis": "server.cjs /api/chat: after input length checks, getBotResponse() tries KB regexes and flows; when nothing matches and OPENAI_API_KEY or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set, the server POSTs the raw user message to the LLM API with a generic system prompt and returns data.choices[0].message.content to the caller. No authGuard, no session validation, no per-user quota — the perRouteRateLimit applied to /api/consent-audit was not applied here, only the global 120/min limiter keyed on req.ip (easily rotated behind Cloudflare/Railway egress or botnets). The state parameter is client-controlled JSON that drives the multi-step sales flow; final lead writes are sanitized via sanitizeLeadPayload/writeChatLead (good), but the LLM path has no such guardrails.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Gate the LLM fallback behind authentication (authGuard) or behind a verified session token issued to the widget after a proof-of-work/captcha challenge. 2. Apply the existing perRouteRateLimit pattern to /api/chat with a small budget (e.g. 10-20 req/min/IP) plus a daily per-IP and global circuit-breaker cap on LLM calls; stop calling the LLM when the cap trips. 3. Harden the system prompt against instruction override and filter/validate LLM output before returning it (block responses that ignore the sales/support domain). 4. Consider removing the free-form LLM fallback entirely and answering with the static KB + contact form, which already covers the widget's sales purpose. 5. Add spend alerting on the OpenAI/Anthropic accounts to detect abuse quickly.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Confirm the endpoint answers KB traffic unauthenticated: POST /api/chat {\"message\":\"pricing\"} returns a sales answer (verified on production). 2. In a deployment with OPENAI_API_KEY or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY configured (production envs reference these variables in server.cjs), send any message that matches no KB regex — e.g. {\"message\":\"Summarize the plot of Hamlet in one paragraph\"} — and the response is a full LLM completion, proving anonymous quota consumption. 3. Send instruction-override prompts (e.g. \"Ignore previous instructions and ...\") to demonstrate prompt-injection through the returned answer. 4. Loop the request from rotating source IPs to show the only throttle is per-IP 120/min, i.e. unbounded aggregate spend.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0005",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Vulnerable dependencies: react-router 7.17.0 (DoS, open redirect, CSRF bypass), postcss 8.5.14 path traversal, ws 8.20.1 DoS\n\nDependency scanning (trivy fs) of package-lock.json identified 9 known vulnerabilities in shipped dependencies. High severity: (1) GHSA-r28c-9q8g-f849 — postcss 8.5.14 path traversal in previous source map auto-loading (fixed 8.5.18); (2) CVE-2026-55685 — react-router 7.17.0 unauthenticated denial of service via inefficient route matching (fixed 7.18.0); (3) GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 — react-router RSC-mode CSRF bypass allowing action execution before the 400 response (fixed 8.3.0); (4) CVE-2026-48779 — ws 8.20.1 denial of service via memory exhaustion from small WebSocket fragments (fixed 8.21.0). Medium: react-router information disclosure via client-side constructor (CVE-2026-53666) and untrusted redirects / open redirect via missing protocol validation and backslash navigation (CVE-2026-53667, CVE-2026-53669 — fixed 7.18.0). Low: body-parser invalid-limit DoS (CVE-2026-12590), esbuild dev-server file read on Windows (GHSA-g7r4-m6w7-qqqr). react-router is a direct production dependency used for all client-side routing, so its routing/redirect flaws are directly exposed in the SPA."
